Michal Brzozowski <rusolis@poczta.fm> wrote in
news:cb6bf0e7-15cf-4b65-aefd-8777b6f8689b@l33g2000pri.googlegroups.com: 

> Fellow Nethackers,
> 
> I'm creating a separate thread, mostly to cut off from the ongoing
> flamewar in the one started by JG Miller.  I agree that anniversaries
> are a good time for reflection so let's try to evaluate the current
> situation of Nethack and its community and what can be done to make
> things better (as opposed to concentrating on what's wrong with the
> Devteam).
> 
> Let's assume for this discussion that 3.4.3 was the final release.  If
> we want to pick up the development of Nethack and make a fork that
> would obsolete the current vanilla, like Nethack did to Hack a few
> decades ago, there are 2 ways we can go.
> 
> 1. Work on the current codebase, adding new features and fixing
> things, basically continuing what slashem and sporkhack had started.

Which certainly *has* been going on and may still be going on. The
difference may be a) lack of warm bodies to do the work b) lack of
motivation/a plan for a new version - in other words a feeling that
nethack's run is over so to speak. I am curious as to what this group was
like say two months before 3.4.1 was released. 

> 2. Break Nethack into pieces and assemble it into a modern OS project,
> not only code wise, but in general.  By that I mean community
> oriented, with a bugtracker, repository, beta releases, etc.

The code tracking/cvs route may very well be being done. It's certainly
simple enough to set up in linux. The difference being only in setting up
a sourceforge webpage really. 

>  Code
> wise that would mean translating into a modern programming language
> like C++, Java, Python, D, etc (most likely object-oriented).

Why OO? it's a game which lends itself fine to C alone. Granted there
*are* lists and other(?) dynamic memory usages in it. They could be
converted to use files or arrays I suppose if one wants to be pedantic
about it being a strongly typed language. The added problem of players
peeking into data files could then be created I suppose. Just because OO
is become "in" is no reason to switch to it. Trying to write database code
in C would be nuts, whereas trying to calculate pi in Lisp would be too.
They have uses and times when they are simply not the best way to do
things. I see no real reason for using OO other than the lists involved.
Certainly nothing to warrant a switch to C++.
> 
> The effort would have to be very strong in order to convince people to
> stop playing 3.4.3 (which isn't a goal by itself, but would mean that
> we have made a new, better Nethack).
> 
Bug fixes are certainly the main reason for them now. Any changes to the
game as well surface with the release of a new version by definition.
There is by definition a lot of carry over between the two. As for
actually creating a new version of nethack and calling it such, that would
cause problems without knowing the truth of the status of it's development
life.


